joe biden is facing what could be the  first major diplomatic crisis of his  presidency  moscow has recalled its ambassador to  the u.s for consultations after biden  confirmed he thought  russian president putin was a killer the  u.s president was speaking in an  interview with abc news  he also promised to hold vladimir putin  accountable for meddling in the u.s  elections  director of national titles this was the  interview that galvanized two huge  nations the u.s and russia  when in an exclusive interview with abc  news george stephanopoulos asked  president joe biden  so you know vladimir putin you think  he's a killer mm-hmm  i do so what price must he pay the price  he's going to pay well you'll see  shortly  the interview comes as the united states  is thought to be preparing new sanctions  against russia for its meddling in the  election  at an earlier meeting biden made a  pointed remark to putin  you said you know he doesn't have a soul  i did say that to him yes  and to end his response was we  understand one another  i wasn't being a wise guy i was alone  with him in his office that's how it  came about it was when  president bush had said i've looked in  his eyes and saw his soul  i said look in your eyes and i don't  think you have a soul and looked back at  me said we understand each other  it's unclear what kind of a response  moscow will have  but no doubt u.s russia relations have  seldom been this low  let's bring in our correspondence  following this story for us we have  emily sherwin in moscow and william  gluecroft here in berlin it's good to  see you both  emily let's start with you there in  moscow joe biden said he considers putin  to be a killer  what exactly is he referring to  well joe biden didn't make it very clear  what exactly he was referring to but it  is clear that over  putin's time in office there have been  uh  a whole list of prominent killings of  kremlin critics or at least  attempted assassinations for example  there's the opposition or the critical  journalist anna paritkovsky who was  killed in 2006.  there was the opposition politician  baris nemtsov who was killed in 2015.  there's the attempted assassination and  poisoning of sergey  skripal in 2018 and last year  the attempted poisoning of opposition  politician alexey navalny as well  now putin's critics have always squarely  blamed him for those killings or at  least his system  of course putin himself has always  denied any ties with uh these killings  and with other killings  um and uh of course investigations as  well have never  linked any of those murders or attempted  murders  to vladimir putin himself okay william  let's talk about this u.s intelligence  report that  essentially blames moscow for meddling  in the u.s elections  to what extent does it say that russia  actually tried to sway the vote  moscow and other countries as well but  when it comes to russia the report  basically outlines  a campaign of misinformation a campaign  of  influence peddling placing misleading  or outright lies into news media into  social media  accounts influential people on social  media  right-wing media also peddling favors  with many trump associates the report  doesn't actually outline or specify at  least the declassified version  doesn't specify any specific examples or  names but one of the biggest  examples being pointed to is trump's  lawyer rudy giuliani meeting with  ukrainian  lawmaker andre derek who has since been  sanctioned by the u.s and has been  accused of basically being an agent of  the russian state  helping put these kind of ideas these  anti-biden ideas a lot of  a lot of these lies we've heard or  misleading facts about  uh biden's and biden's family's role in  ukraine and corruption allegations none  of which have  and have been any evidence has been  found to that effect but putting those  kinds of ideas those kinds of stories  out there into the u.s media and social  media to cause confusion  to stir up trouble we saw that in 2016.  one major difference was in 2016 it  appeared that russia also tried to  actually hack  election infrastructure and that was not  the case in 2020 they maintained they  maintained more of a  influence and social media and  misinformation campaign  so emily we know that the kremlin has  broadly rejected accusations of election  interference but have they addressed any  of the  specific allegations in this report  no there was a pretty standard denial  that we heard from the kremlin  spokesperson dimitri piskov yesterday  he called that report wrong baseless  and unsubstantiated and basically  questioned  the quality of the claims in the report  but i think the really big news here in  russia isn't so much the report that's  sort of seen as  the usual accusations that are leveled  um  at the kremlin and at russia um that the  kremlin has denied over the years  the real news here is this statement  about putin being  a killer one parliamentarian today  said that that is really a red line that  the u.s  has now crossed and the next step would  only be for  russia and the u.s to break diplomatic  ties you have to understand that this is  essentially a personal insult that  has been leveled um at the russian  president in the country where even  putin's critics usually kind of make  broad claims or  broad criticisms against the kremlin or  against the government instead of  touching the president himself  yeah president joe biden certainly  talking tough there william just quickly  if you can  he says that there will be consequences  for russia what exactly are we talking  about here  he was very vague as we heard in that  report partly because he doesn't want to  probably tip his national security hand  but also there's  rather limited room that biden and the  united states can  can maneuver for punishing russia  sanctions are usually the go-to  a tool we there's many sanctions against  russia and russian individuals with  varying effects it's quite easy for many  russians especially wealthy russians  well-connected russians to  evade those sanctions but u.s officials  are probably also going to look at other  kinds of financial tools  russian money sloshing around in western  financial institutions that they can  maybe go after  and of course building alliances keeping  united front with the eu  with nato to present a united front with  russia that would make it harder for  russia to  enact this kind of divide and conquer  campaign that we've seen over the last  several years  chipping away at u.s allies especially  in eastern europe  our correspondents william gluckhoff  here in berlin emily sherwin  in moscow thank you both very much
 

